 under increasing pressure from the Crown of Aragon, which sought to consolidate its dominion over Sardinia. His twelve-year rule was a pivotal, though often understated, period of maintaining Aragonese recognition of Arborean autonomy, laying the groundwork for the more confrontational policies of his successor, Marianus IV.

During his reign, Peter III largely adhered to the established foreign policy of his father, which involved a delicate balance of fealty to the Crown of Aragon while safeguarding Arborean sovereignty. He continued to manage the internal affairs of the Judicate, focusing on administrative stability and maintaining the intricate feudal relationships that defined the state. Although his tenure was not marked by large-scale military campaigns against Aragon, the constant diplomatic maneuvering and the strategic marriages of his siblings, such as that of his sister Constance to the Doria family, were crucial in preserving the Judicate's tenuous independence.

Peter III's legacy is primarily one of continuity and quiet resilience. By successfully navigating the immediate aftermath of his father's death and preventing an Aragonese annexation during a period of relative weakness, he ensured the survival of the Judicate of Arborea. This period of sustained, if cautious, independence provided a stable foundation for his more ambitious and militarily adept brother, Marianus IV, who would later challenge Aragonese hegemony directly, transforming the island's political landscape. Peter’s reign thus bridged a critical gap, ensuring the Judicate's survival through a generation."}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-31T08:43:01Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#41183576199", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1250"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": "1250"}], "newValue": ["The succession of Hugh II, Peter III's father, left the Judicate of Arborea in a delicate balance of independence and Aragonese suzerainty.", "The ongoing expansionist ambitions of the Crown of Aragon exerted continuous pressure on the remaining independent Sardinian states.", "The intricate system of feudal allegiances and rivalries among Sardinian noble families and external powers created a complex political environment."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-31T08:43:01Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#41183576199", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1347"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1347"}], "newValue": ["Peter III's reign ensured the continued, albeit fragile, independence of the Judicate of Arborea from Aragonese direct rule for over a decade.", 